meta {
  name: BerekenK3ProRataPrecisie
  type: http
  seq: 11
}

post {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/web/btw/bereken
  body: json
  auth: none
}

headers {
  Content-Type: application/json
  Accept: application/json
}

body:json {
  {
    "regels": [
      { "btwKode": "3", "bedrag": 100.00, "totaalRecupel": 0 },
      { "btwKode": "1", "bedrag": 50.00, "totaalRecupel": 0 }
    ],
    "swRegime": true,
    "swPrijsRecup": false,
    "typeKorting": "K3",
    "documentKortingPercentage": 0,
    "documentKortingBedrag": 15
  }
}

assert {
  res.status: eq 200
  res.body.totaal.regimeToegepast: eq true
  res.body.totaal.totaalBasis: eq 135
  res.body.totaal.totBtw: eq 21.6
  res.body.totaal.totaalInclBtw: eq 156.6
  res.body.groepen: length 2
  res.body.groepen[0].btwKode: eq '1'
  res.body.groepen[0].basis: eq 45
  res.body.groepen[0].btwBedrag: eq 2.7
  res.body.groepen[1].btwKode: eq '3'
  res.body.groepen[1].basis: eq 90
  res.body.groepen[1].btwBedrag: eq 18.9
}

docs {
  Regression test for bug fixed 2026-09-28: the K3 pro-rata split used to
  leave ttBtwGroep.basis/btwBedrag fully unrounded, so this exact payload
  used to come back with floating-point noise on the group rows
  (basis 89.9999999995/45.0000000005, btwBedrag 18.8999999999/2.7 instead
  of clean values) even though totaal.* (already ROUND(...,2)) looked
  fine. FindBtwKodeGroep now rounds basis/btwBedrag to 4 decimals before
  writing the temp-table row.

  Raw basis: 100 (btw-kode "3" = 21%) + 50 (btw-kode "1" = 6%) = 150.
  K3 documentKortingBedrag=15 split pro-rata by group share:
  reductie_3 = 15 * (100/150) = 10 -> basis_3 = 90.00, btwBedrag_3 = 18.90
  reductie_1 = 15 * (50/150)  = 5  -> basis_1 = 45.00, btwBedrag_1 = 2.70
  totaalBasis = 90 + 45 = 135, totBtw = 18.90 + 2.70 = 21.60,
  totaalInclBtw = 156.60. groepen is ordered by ttGroepAgg's unique index
  on btwKode (alphabetical "1" before "3"), not input/insertion order -
  confirmed live against MarcTest 2026-09-28. The per-group assertions
  above confirm no floating-point noise leaks through (exactly 90/18.9,
  not 89.9999999995/18.8999999999).
}
